<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syriac Christians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> Christians are the Christian communities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> whose liturgy and literature use Syriac, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. They include the Syriac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a>. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> they formed a large part of the population under Muslim dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syriac-christians.jpg" alt="Syriac Christians" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Holy-Sepulchre_Jesus-Detail-01.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
